24
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.489
John Talburt: So, with that…

25
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.950
John Talburt: Let's continue the lecture. So last time, we talked about the seven Principles of information quality.

26
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:39.380
John Talburt: And the differentiation between… that I make, anyway, and in this program, we make a differentiation between information quality and data quality.

27
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.499
John Talburt: Data quality, basically, is do data meet data requirements?

28
00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:51.659
John Talburt: And so, we're looking at the condition of the data. But, as I mentioned earlier.

29
00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:57.519
John Talburt: You can have data that's in really good condition, it's meeting all the requirements you set for.

30
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.820
John Talburt: But when you use that data to build an information product or service.

31
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:13.589
John Talburt: It's not necessarily a quality product or service if it doesn't fit the needs, fill the needs of the users of that information, product, or service.

32
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.090
John Talburt: So, again, as Tom Redman likes to say.

33
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:26.850
John Talburt: Is the data right? In other words, does the data fit your requirements? But is it the right data for what you're trying to do?

34
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.829
John Talburt: So we're gonna investigate both of those for the file that, just put out there.

35
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:36.760
John Talburt: So, anyway, we talked about those principles, and…

36
00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:42.090
John Talburt: Again, in our definition of information quality, the key term

37
00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:52.509
John Talburt: is value. So, what we're trying to do is we're trying to maximize value, minimize risk for the data assets that are in an organization.

38
00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.199
John Talburt: And at the same time, we're trying to produce value for the users of that information.

39
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.080
John Talburt: So, it's really all about value.

40
00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:08.620
John Talburt: And of course, along with that, as I mentioned, Annette McGilvery likes to use the term trust, but those go hand in hand, right?

41
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.910
John Talburt: If you, if you have,

42
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:28.710
John Talburt: an information product or service that's producing value for you, then you… you need to be able to trust that in order for… if it's producing value, it gives you trust. If you trust it, it helps you produce value. So, they kind of go hand in hand, but we're going with the value proposition.

43
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.679
John Talburt: So, so let's talk a little bit, then, about the value.

44
00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.679
John Talburt: And so, as I just mentioned, information quality

45
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.859
John Talburt: It's about relating information to value.

46
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.890
John Talburt: And again, this is our,

47
00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:57.789
John Talburt: Definition that we use, maximizing the value of an organization's information assets, and making sure that the information products and services are valuable to the people that use them.

48
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:04.450
John Talburt: So, there's a couple of ways to relate information to value.

49
00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.860
John Talburt: And…

50
00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:23.579
John Talburt: I like to use pictures for this. So, in any organization, you're going to have all info… I think it's virtually true that every organization that I can think of uses data. So there's some use of data in any organization.

51
00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.970
John Talburt: And any organization is going to have some kind of problems.

52
00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:40.249
John Talburt: But since generally there's an intersection there, it may be true that some problems are not related directly to data. Maybe they're cultural problems, they're having problems with communication.

53
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:47.410
John Talburt: But in general, we… what we're looking for is the intersection between business problems

54
00:05:47.450 --> 00:06:03.389
John Talburt: And the data that's running the business, or the… I say business, but it could be an organization, right? We talked about the three value propositions. There's for-profit, that would be a business. But there's also quality of life, like in a hospital or research center.

55
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:16.379
John Talburt: And then there's also mission accomplishment. So, I want to talk generally about organizations and their value. So, the value might be not necessarily money, but it could be in accomplishing their given mission.

56
00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:23.750
John Talburt: But anyway, so what we want to investigate is… say you go into an organization, you're graduating, you're now…

57
00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.449
John Talburt: Data quality analyst, and you've been hired by this company.

58
00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:34.720
John Talburt: And so, they're having problems, so… so the first step is this.

59
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:43.739
John Talburt: We need to understand what's the intersection between the data and those problems, and so this gives us two ways to go about it.

60
00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:49.070
John Talburt: So one way is that we can start with a business issue.

61
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:55.620
John Talburt: Okay, every organization has some kind of an issue that it's facing. Then the first step would be

62
00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:03.060
John Talburt: does… is it related to the data in the organization? And it may not be, but oftentimes that it is.

63
00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:17.159
John Talburt: So… so one way to start is you go to the managers, you go to the executives, and they know, okay, we're having this problem, we're not getting our invoices out.

64
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:26.800
John Talburt: to the customers properly, or we're not getting payments, we're, we're not… we have a problem retaining customers. We have some kind of a business issue.

65
00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.100
John Talburt: then…

66
00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:37.200
John Talburt: the next step would be to look, and is there a cause, is there a root cause of this problem in the data? Is something in the data causing this?

67
00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:45.899
John Talburt: Once I know that, then I can improve the data quality, right? So again, we're making the distinction between information quality and data quality.

68
00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:52.129
John Talburt: There's something wrong with the condition of it's not meeting the requirements that are needed to produce.

69
00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.160
John Talburt: To solve the problem that we're having.

70
00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:02.069
John Talburt: If it's the invoicing, maybe the addresses are wrong, maybe they're out of date, maybe they're incomplete.

71
00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:11.020
John Talburt: So, once we investigate… we know it's related to data, and we investigate the quality of the data in terms of the meeting requirements.

72
00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.779
John Talburt: Does it have all the information that we need?

73
00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:19.189
John Talburt: Then, we can impr… once we improve that quality, we can improve the business results.

74
00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:26.300
John Talburt: So, this is one way of looking at the issue, is starting with the business issue.

75
00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:35.430
John Talburt: Seeing if it's related to data, solving the data problem, and then, hopefully addressing the business issue.

76
00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.589
John Talburt: That's one way to do it. The other way to do it

77
00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:45.130
John Talburt: is we start with a data quality assessment. In other words, we go in, you're new.

78
00:08:45.490 --> 00:09:00.329
John Talburt: nobody's given you any predictor problem yet, but you look at all the data systems that are running, and you say, okay, well, what's the condition of the data? Do we have data quality requirements for the data? Are the data really meeting those requirements?

79
00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:08.210
John Talburt: So you could start with the data quality assessment and say, are the data really meeting the requirements that we've set for the data?

80
00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:14.090
John Talburt: And if we discover that there are data quality issues, that is.

81
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.459
John Talburt: The data are not in alignment with our requirements.

82
00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.169
John Talburt: Then we can address those, and then,

83
00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:32.889
John Talburt: Well, first, then we say… this is very important, by the way, I didn't mean to skip this step. Once we find that there's some data quality anomalies.

84
00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:42.959
John Talburt: That does not automatically mean that there's a business problem. I can guarantee you I can go into any organization that has a relational database system.

85
00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.869
John Talburt: and find many tables that are completely null. There's nothing in them.

86
00:09:48.630 --> 00:10:03.270
John Talburt: But it's not necessarily impacting the business because they're not using that data. They're not using those columns, okay? So, the very important step is once we discover that data has some kind of anomaly, it's not meeting requirements.

87
00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.810
John Talburt: The next step is to determine then, is that having an impact on the business?

88
00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.450
John Talburt: It's the opposite of what we said before.

89
00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:20.859
John Talburt: Is bad data causing a problem with the business? And if it is, of course, then we can improve the data quality and improve our business results.

90
00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:25.590
John Talburt: So this happens oftentimes in both ways.

91
00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:36.680
John Talburt: In the old days, they used to say, when it was very data quality oriented, they would say, okay, we have all these data quality requirements, we go in and we assess all of our data.

92
00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:54.370
John Talburt: But that can be very time-consuming and labor-intensive to go in and assess all the data when not necessarily you'll find… I mean, you may find problems, but even those data quality problems may not have any impact on the business or on the organization.

93
00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.230
John Talburt: So,

94
00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:14.710
John Talburt: I'm not advising you to go either in any particular way, it could work either way, but oftentimes it's most fruitful to start with a business impact, something that's going wrong with the organization that you can relate to data. Then that gives you a specific target of things to fix.

95
00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:19.669
John Talburt: If you just go sweeping through the data looking for data quality problems.

96
00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:26.770
John Talburt: That's good, but you want to hold back and not say, you know, well, we want all perfect data in our systems.

97
00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:37.880
John Talburt: Because, again, making it perfect is not necessarily going to improve your business results if the data are not involved in the business or in some significant part of the business.

98
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.860
John Talburt: So, it's good to look at this, in both ways.

99
00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:53.270
John Talburt: So, these are the two views of how to relate information to value.

100
00:11:53.660 --> 00:12:03.350
John Talburt: Starting with a business problem that's related to data, that you can fix the data to improve the business, or you can look for defects in the data.

101
00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:09.580
John Talburt: determine if those defects are impacting the business. If they are, you can fix them and improve business results.

102
00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:14.200
John Talburt: So there's lots of places to find information valuable.

103
00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:20.360
John Talburt: Obviously, we think often of financial, like the examples of the invoices going out.

104
00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:40.480
John Talburt: But… and that can, affect, you know, operating costs, revenue, cash flow, charges, budget, compliance. This is a big area. Many organizations, have external, agencies that require them to comply to certain,

105
00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:53.030
John Talburt: requirements. For example, we have that here at the university. We have to report everything into the Arkansas Department of Education. We're getting ready to make a big report now that,

106
00:12:53.200 --> 00:13:11.679
John Talburt: we're… you see the final enrollments in our courses, we have to report all that. So we have a lot of regulation related to education. Certainly, that's true in medical, but especially in financial organizations, you have a lot of issues that have to be reported.

107
00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.919
John Talburt: government, industry, privacy.

108
00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:21.909
John Talburt: confidence, trust, so this, this gets back to the,

109
00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.950
John Talburt: Issue, or not the issue, but the…

110
00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:30.670
John Talburt: Danette McGilver is, that raises, that you're really trying to build

111
00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:33.829
John Talburt: Trustworthy data in your organization.

112
00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:40.119
John Talburt: People trust it. If they don't trust it, they won't use it, and if it's not used, it won't create any value.

113
00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.060
John Talburt: So, as I mentioned last time.

114
00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:50.549
John Talburt: You know, we say that, you have to be careful how you say it. I said it wrong to Doug Laney one time, is that

115
00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:58.830
John Talburt: Data only produces value when it's used, okay?

116
00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:07.380
John Talburt: And so the key word there is produce. Data has value even when you're not using it. It has potential value.

117
00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:11.660
John Talburt: But the value is only realized when you actually use it for something.

118
00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:16.049
John Talburt: Like, making decisions, or controlling systems.

119
00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:25.429
John Talburt: Forecasting, that can be a very important thing that you do with data, as well as just general reporting. And then satisfaction, you know.

120
00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:31.040
John Talburt: If you're using… you're in a business, especially where you have customers.

121
00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:36.880
John Talburt: Then, you know, customers are at the top of the… Pyramid.

122
00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:45.439
John Talburt: They need to be… if you want to be successful, you need to have good customer satisfaction, what they call customer experience.

123
00:14:45.820 --> 00:15:03.989
John Talburt: And data has a lot to do with that, right? So, I don't know if you've had this experience of going into a website and trying to order something or buy something, and you go through a bunch of pages and things, and they ask you things that you don't know exactly how to answer. Maybe you have that experience with Workday trying to register.

124
00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:08.240
John Talburt: I know I have a lot of bad experiences with work.

125
00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:16.169
John Talburt: So, that's poor customer experience, or poor customer satisfaction. And oftentimes, that's related to data.

126
00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:21.450
John Talburt: Suppliers, employees, so there are many ways that you can find

127
00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:26.070
John Talburt: Inf… use information to create value.

128
00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:30.390
John Talburt: As we mentioned, your risk, you want to minimize risk.

129
00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:33.710
John Talburt: You want to meet all the regulatory requirements.

130
00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:36.380
John Talburt: credit, investment.

131
00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:45.329
John Talburt: Competitiveness, this is another thing that's sort of an intangible, but it's an extremely important value, is to be…

132
00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:53.059
John Talburt: part of customer satisfaction. How are you competing with other organizations that are in your same field?

133
00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:57.410
John Talburt: And again, data can have an important part in that.

134
00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:03.550
John Talburt: Leakage. Of course, this is what a lot of people think about immediately when we think about risk.

135
00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:10.870
John Talburt: is, we have sensitive data that gets out of our control, right?

136
00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:13.799
John Talburt: Hackers get in still.

137
00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.500
John Talburt: It was often said that there's two kinds of companies.

138
00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:26.260
John Talburt: Those companies That have had their personality or customer information exposed, or hacked.

139
00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.050
John Talburt: And those that don't know it yet.

140
00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:36.780
John Talburt: That everybody, to some extent, has been hacked or leaked information that they wanted to keep private.

141
00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:44.950
John Talburt: Productivity, we often think about now, in the age of AI, this is the key word, right?

142
00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:49.930
John Talburt: We want to use these large language models

143
00:16:50.140 --> 00:17:07.180
John Talburt: to improve our workflows through agents, right? So Agentic AI is the big thing right now. And agents are like little employees inside of the LLM that can do work, and so we can be potentially more productive through data.

144
00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:14.829
John Talburt: Namely, through these, Large language models, which require some kind of contact, which is also data.

145
00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:21.830
John Talburt: And then… Health, we talked about the other value propositions.

146
00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:28.750
John Talburt: In healthcare disease control treatments, Mental, treatment, diet, fitness.

147
00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:33.349
John Talburt: Safety, this is another big area, right? We're seeing a lot of that now.

148
00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.779
John Talburt: And… and environmental protection.

149
00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.200
John Talburt: safety issues.

150
00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:51.740
John Talburt: Accident prevention. A lot of that goes along with now we have self-driving cars, we're trying to make things safer, we're trying to study traffic flows, we're trying to assess the safety of

151
00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:59.670
John Talburt: of automobiles. So, everywhere you look, there's data involved, and you… if you're making the right

152
00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:07.379
John Talburt: making that data into the right information, products, and services, you can create value. So there are many, many places to find it.

153
00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:12.899
John Talburt: Now, So, just as an aside.

154
00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:23.950
John Talburt: If value is a very simple formula, right? So, value is the difference Between benefits and cost.

155
00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:32.869
John Talburt: So if I'm gonna undertake some enterprise, which is doing something, I'm gonna do something, there's some cost involved.

156
00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:36.390
John Talburt: It could be money, it could be time.

157
00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:42.009
John Talburt: Could be wear and tear, could be a lot of things. There's something you have to give

158
00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:54.360
John Talburt: in terms of cost, to get some… to do your action, whatever it is you're trying to do. And the idea is that when you get finished doing it, like building a product, or…

159
00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:59.369
John Talburt: You know, you're, you're gonna fix up your house, you're gonna do something.

160
00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:11.009
John Talburt: It's gonna cost you something to do that, but the idea is you're gonna realize some benefit out of it. It'll give you some benefit. And of course, what we would like is for that benefit

161
00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:20.980
John Talburt: the value of that, or that benefit, to be greater than the cost that it required to get that benefit, and that's what the value is. The value's the difference.

162
00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:27.009
John Talburt: And so, we have a very simple formula for measuring that. We call it return on investment.

163
00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:40.459
John Talburt: And it applies to all kinds of data issues, financial issues, medical issues, whatever endeavor that you'd want to think about, we can think of it in terms of return on investment.

164
00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:45.209
John Talburt: And it's the net gain calculated as a percentage of the amount invested.

165
00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:57.029
John Talburt: In other words, what was my gain that I got? How much were the benefits above the cost? And then divide that by the cost, and that gives me my return on investment.

166
00:19:57.490 --> 00:20:03.640
John Talburt: So the return on investment expressed as a percentage, we multiply by 100 to get percentage.

167
00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:09.719
John Talburt: Is the net gain divided by the cost that it took to get that net gain?

168
00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:18.540
John Talburt: So, we always want that to be positive, right? So why would we want to do something? Invest time, money, all types of costs.

169
00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:30.919
John Talburt: and get a benefit that was smaller than the cost that required to get that benefit. We always want the benefit to be larger than the cost to get the benefit, and that would be a positive ROI.

170
00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:32.870
John Talburt: So,

171
00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:41.760
John Talburt: And it's a good way to compare alternative opportunities. So, in business, we use the return on investment all the time, because typically we have

172
00:20:42.020 --> 00:21:00.320
John Talburt: many opportunities to improve things, right? And we can't do everything at once, so we have to make decisions about, do we want to do Improvement A, or improvement B, or improvement C? And so one way to help evaluate is calculate the return on investments of each one of those opportunities.

173
00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:09.400
John Talburt: So, also, if we're looking at anything long-term, Longer than a year.

174
00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:14.190
John Talburt: Then we really should use discounted values for cost and revenue.

175
00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:16.570
John Talburt: And so, what do we mean by that?

176
00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:22.240
John Talburt: So, if I can get a 5% annual return on my investment.

177
00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:27.450
John Talburt: What is today's present value of a $100 payment?

178
00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:30.150
John Talburt: To me, one year from now, so…

179
00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:38.150
John Talburt: The idea of an annual return on investment is that money should be working all the time.

180
00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:46.679
John Talburt: Money shouldn't be setting idle in a… in a savings account. Well, not… that's not good… under your mattress at home.

181
00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:50.350
John Talburt: Just sitting there, because money needs to be working.

182
00:21:50.420 --> 00:22:07.949
John Talburt: You need to be investing it, so it's… so if we have some general idea of what the working value of money should be, in this case we're calling it annual return, say it's 5%. So the way to think about this, suppose that somebody says, okay, I'm going to pay you $100,

183
00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:11.119
John Talburt: But I'm not gonna pay it until one year from now.

184
00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.690
John Talburt: So the question becomes.

185
00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:19.470
John Talburt: what's the value of $100 one year from now? So we have to back up and say, well.

186
00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:31.109
John Talburt: what would I need to… how much money would I need today so that it would grow to $100 at a 5% interest rate? So that's a pretty easy one to solve.

187
00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.900
John Talburt: You know, how much money do I need today

188
00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:41.890
John Talburt: to have $100 at the end of one year. And of course, it's very easy to solve. We say, well, I've got,

189
00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:51.149
John Talburt: the X that I'm looking for is the amount of money I need today, but… but that money plus 5% gain in that money

190
00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:55.219
John Talburt: I want, in one year, to equal $100.

191
00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:59.759
John Talburt: So, it's a simple algebraic formula.

192
00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:05.240
John Talburt: I'm basically gonna take $100 and divide it by 1.05,

193
00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:10.610
John Talburt: And so that tells me that, basically, $100 a year from now.

194
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:18.959
John Talburt: It's only worth $95 today if my annual growth of money is expected to be at least 5%.

195
00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:22.179
John Talburt: Okay? So that's called the discounted value.

196
00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:30.210
John Talburt: Another place where that comes in… well, we'll talk about that in a minute. So, we call that the net present value of money.

197
00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:45.100
John Talburt: In other words, again, the basic assumption is that money should constantly be growing in value, and we call that the annual discount rate, like 5% in the previous example. And it will vary from industry to industry, or type of business.

198
00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:48.930
John Talburt: But you should have some idea of the annual discount rate.

199
00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:54.790
John Talburt: Because uninvested money loses value. Put the money in your,

200
00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.700
John Talburt: Safety deposit box or under your mattress at home.

201
00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:08.640
John Talburt: Then it's basically losing, because if you could, on the average, get 5% return, or even 3% return in a savings account.

202
00:24:08.750 --> 00:24:14.360
John Talburt: That's where it should be, working and getting… and gaining money over time, or gaining value over time.

203
00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:19.600
John Talburt: So, future dollars are worth less today, is the basic idea.

204
00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:25.899
John Talburt: So here's where this comes up a lot. I don't know how many of you play the lotto or play the

205
00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:33.850
John Talburt: These, jackpot prizes. But if you read the fine print, here's how it goes. Hey!

206
00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:43.570
John Talburt: play the lotto, and if you win, you'll get a million dollars, okay? One million dollar jackpot prize. But then you read the fine print, and it says, but…

207
00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:54.120
John Talburt: What happens, if you win the lotto, yes, we're gonna give you $100,000 today, And then $100,000

208
00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:56.469
John Talburt: A year for 9 years.

209
00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:12.379
John Talburt: $100,000 today, then another payment next year of $100,000, then the year after that, $100,000, and so forth. That's your… and so it's a million dollars, that's what they say. Well, it is, if you just look at it, but if you put time into it, it's not worth a million dollars.

210
00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:18.220
John Talburt: Let's just suppose, for argument, that the discount rate is 10%.

211
00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:25.989
John Talburt: Pretty high, but… Say that money could, in general, gain 10% a year in value.

212
00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:36.200
John Talburt: Then that prize money, a million dollars, paid out like that over 9 years, Is only worth $675,000 today.

213
00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:47.589
John Talburt: And that, by the way, there are many companies that are engaged in this, right? If you win one of these lotteries where they have that, where you're required to take the money out in payments.

214
00:25:47.860 --> 00:26:01.139
John Talburt: Then there are many companies around that will come in and say, hey, I saw that you won this, we'll give you the cash today. And this is how they figure out how much cash to give you. So their business would be, say you won this.

215
00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:10.360
John Talburt: I'd say, okay, well, don't worry, we'll give you $500,000 today, but you give us the annuity. In other words, you give us

216
00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:20.189
John Talburt: Those 9 years of payments, and we'll take those payments, and we'll… but we'll give you, the discounted value minus some profit today.

217
00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:33.039
John Talburt: So, by the way, there's, thank God for Excel, right? So, Excel has a nice formula for net present value that's called NPV.

218
00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:43.339
John Talburt: And so what we can do is, we can do it this way. We can say, okay, the first $100,000 is $100,000 because I got it today.

219
00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:49.809
John Talburt: But then I can say, I go into my Excel formula of NPV and say, okay.

220
00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:54.739
John Talburt: My annual growth rate is 10%, so that's the 0.1.

221
00:26:55.080 --> 00:27:01.140
John Talburt: And then I just followed that with a series of payments for each year, so there's $100,000 for the first

222
00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:03.250
John Talburt: At the end of the second year.

223
00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:08.349
John Talburt: The third year, fourth year, so there's 9 of those. I just plugged those into that formula.

224
00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:12.550
John Talburt: And it comes out, that's how I get the $675,000.

225
00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:14.479
John Talburt: The current value.

226
00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:35.070
John Talburt: Then there's… that one is the one where if I have uneven payments, that I need to list what payment it is. But if all the payments are the same, like this, all the payments are the same amount, $100,000, I can use a different Excel formula called PV, present value, and I can just say this. I can say.

227
00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:42.570
John Talburt: Okay, the discount rate is 10% again. There's 10 payments of $100,000.

228
00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:50.240
John Talburt: And then the zero says that I don't count the first one. The first one is an immediate payment, just like in my previous formula.

229
00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:59.190
John Talburt: And so, when I do that, I get the same result, but I don't have to list out each individual payment, because all the payments are the same.

230
00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:07.299
John Talburt: Everybody on board with the present value of money?

231
00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:11.520
John Talburt: Alright, so here's an example.

232
00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:15.460
John Talburt: So, there's a not-for-profit company.

233
00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:27.600
John Talburt: And they have a problem with inconsistent reference tables across their operating unit, and it's creating a real cost in correcting erroneous shipments all the time, because their reference tables are bad.

234
00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:34.149
John Talburt: So, they have a solution. They want to set up a single point of truth, what we call master data.

235
00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:37.299
John Talburt: Or SPOT, single point of truth.

236
00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:42.230
John Talburt: Corporate reference, so that every… everybody uses the same reference data.

237
00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:50.840
John Talburt: And they do some analysis, and the cost… it's gonna take 4 years to implement this fully. It's a very large company.

238
00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:55.429
John Talburt: And it'll… the cost will be $120,000 a year.

239
00:28:56.290 --> 00:29:02.449
John Talburt: The benefits over 4 years, are gonna be $160,000.

240
00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:23.209
John Talburt: So, if I just look at that without bringing time into consideration, my benefit is I'm going to benefit $160,000, it's going to cost me $120,000, so if I just use my simple return on investment, I take the $160,000 minus the $120, that's my net benefit.

241
00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:29.960
John Talburt: and divide that by the cost it takes to get it, and so I get a 33% return on my investment.

242
00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:36.549
John Talburt: That's just looking at it without time value over 4 years. That's just saying.

243
00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:43.560
John Talburt: money now, in the present. But let's look at… it'll become a little more complicated here.

244
00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:53.640
John Talburt: Because that… the money that I spend, the cost, and the benefits that I get don't necessarily come in an even way.

245
00:29:53.810 --> 00:30:00.710
John Talburt: So, for example, in one scenario, I have to spend right off the bat.

246
00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:07.410
John Talburt: $45,000 in the first year, but it's only going to benefit me $10,000 in the first year.

247
00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:13.780
John Talburt: But in the second year, I have to… I can get to spend less, 35,000.

248
00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:17.700
John Talburt: And then the second year, I'll get a $20,000 benefit.

249
00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:30.009
John Talburt: And then, in the third year, I'd only have to spend $25,000, and I get a $40,000 benefit, and in the fourth year, I only spend $15,000, and I get a $90,000 benefit.

250
00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:40.180
John Talburt: That scenario is the same overall in that my total expenditures is $120, and my total benefit is $160.

251
00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:50.300
John Talburt: But each… the pay… the benefit that I get each year and the costs that I have each year are not constant, okay?

252
00:30:50.800 --> 00:31:03.139
John Talburt: And then I look at the second scenario, then it varies again, and I say, okay, well, my first year, I have to spend $45,000, but right away, I get a $90,000 benefit.

253
00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:08.839
John Talburt: And then the next year, I spend $35,000, and I get a $40,000 benefit.

254
00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:13.100
John Talburt: In this one, I'm seeing, I'm sort of getting… diminishing returns.

255
00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:29.730
John Talburt: So, and then the third year, I spend $25 and I only get $20 back. In the third year… fourth year, I spend 15 and get $10 back. Again, overall, it's the same cost and benefit. That all adds up to $120,000 in cost.

256
00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.940
John Talburt: And $160,000 in benefit.

257
00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:47.109
John Talburt: And then finally, the third scenario is sort of the opposite. My costs are very low at the beginning, and my benefits are high, but it goes the other way. So, year one, it's $15,000 costs $90,000 benefit.

258
00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:56.839
John Talburt: 25 in the next year of my cost, and then 40, so forth and so on. Again, all 3 of these scenarios are $120,000 in cost.

259
00:31:56.980 --> 00:32:01.819
John Talburt: And, $160,000 in benefit, but…

260
00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:08.130
John Talburt: the return on investment, because if I take present value into account.

261
00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:13.809
John Talburt: then I can see this, that in Scenario 1,

262
00:32:14.020 --> 00:32:19.449
John Talburt: Again, all the returns without using time are 33%.

263
00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:34.239
John Talburt: But, if I take the net present value, they vary dramatically. So, in scenario one, my net present value for that project is only 26% return on investment.

264
00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:43.109
John Talburt: Whereas in Scenario 2, it's a 36% return on investment. In Scenario 3, it's 40%.

265
00:32:43.490 --> 00:32:47.030
John Talburt: And we can see the reason why, right? Look in Scenario 1.

266
00:32:47.060 --> 00:33:05.479
John Talburt: most of my costs are closer in time. My high costs are now, and my benefits are farther out, and those benefits farther out are worthless to me now, when I'm planning this project. So, Scenario 1 has the lowest return on investment using net present value.

267
00:33:05.720 --> 00:33:11.290
John Talburt: Because I have upfront costs and outs, and my benefits are farther out.

268
00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:16.620
John Talburt: Which is exactly the opposite of Scenario 3, where I have my cost

269
00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:33.379
John Talburt: come later, and so those costs are worth less now. They have less value now because they're farther out in time, but I'm getting my benefits now, early on in the early years. So that means now my return on investment is actually much larger. It's 40%.

270
00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:41.620
John Talburt: Whereas, where they're pretty much even in Scenario 2, my costs, my benefits will pretty much go linearly and hand in hand.

271
00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:47.089
John Talburt: It's very close to, the actual, if I did without time.

272
00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:54.630
John Talburt: Without using time value, it's 33%. In Scenario 2, it's just 36%. So it's a little better.

273
00:33:55.300 --> 00:34:06.480
John Talburt: So this gives you, then, the reason that in looking at any kind of a long-term project, you really need to look at the present value of money to get a good assessment of what's going on.

274
00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:11.310
John Talburt: And this is all done assuming an annual discount rate of 5%.

275
00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:17.459
John Talburt: Any questions or comments on them?

276
00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:23.020
John Talburt: You'll have an exercise like that that you can be able to do.

277
00:34:23.650 --> 00:34:27.790
John Talburt: So, making the business case for information quality.

278
00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:35.400
John Talburt: So, it's really important to have that business case, right? Because if we think that information quality

279
00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:39.840
John Talburt: I hope you think that, because it's part of our curriculum.

280
00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:47.870
John Talburt: is value, then we need to make a business case for. We want to think of information quality

281
00:34:48.070 --> 00:35:02.790
John Talburt: the way people think about it right now is it's just about cleaning up dirty data. It's just like the janitor has to come in and clean up the floor. There's no benefit, there's no value to it other than it's just something that needs to be done.

282
00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:18.529
John Talburt: But we want to change that thinking to people that think about information quality and improving quality as an investment, right? Yeah, we're going to spend some money to do it, but the benefits that we get out of it are going to be larger than that cost.

283
00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:20.960
John Talburt: So that's what a business case does.

284
00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:25.549
John Talburt: It, it, it needs to be,

285
00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:29.310
John Talburt: A way to say that information quality improvement

286
00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:32.169
John Talburt: We'll be successful and create value.

287
00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:35.160
John Talburt: And so, it will help you,

288
00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:44.310
John Talburt: get people to thinking about information quality improvement as an investment, and not just an added expense. Because even though there's expense.

289
00:35:44.490 --> 00:35:47.589
John Talburt: It will return back more value than it costs.

290
00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:51.660
John Talburt: And that helps you gain support from management.

291
00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:57.479
John Talburt: And that helps you get the resources you need to have a successful project, so they all work together.

292
00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:08.409
John Talburt: So, in order to do that, you really need to, have… understand what are the business goals, or the organizational goals. I keep using business, I probably should change this.

293
00:36:08.510 --> 00:36:18.809
John Talburt: Because, again, it's true for business, but it's also true for healthcare, it's true for government organizations, nonprofit organizations.

294
00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:22.080
John Talburt: They have goals, they have things they're trying to accomplish.

295
00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:24.130
John Talburt: And you need to align with that.

296
00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:28.110
John Talburt: And so, you need to make a clear connection between

297
00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:35.759
John Talburt: The information quality improvement, and achieving those business… those goals and objectives of the organization.

298
00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:42.990
John Talburt: And in order to do that, and this is something I hope that you will take away from this course.

299
00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:51.150
John Talburt: is what we call the information quality context. So again, it's not just about data. I mean, data's super important.

300
00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:55.710
John Talburt: And we want to process data into some information product or service.

301
00:36:56.130 --> 00:37:02.100
John Talburt: But in addition to the data, there are 3 other things that we have to be aware of and take into consideration.

302
00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:08.969
John Talburt: In general, but also in particular in our business case. We have to understand The data, of course.

303
00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:17.649
John Talburt: What's in it? What data items are the most important. We need to really understand the data we're using. But there are 3 other very important things.

304
00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:27.989
John Talburt: Well, in understanding the data, one of the things we often look at is what are the critical data elements? Some… some elements of the data are more important than others.

305
00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:31.380
John Talburt: And we call those CDEs, or Critical Data Elements.

306
00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:40.889
John Talburt: And the way we can tell if something is critical is what would happen to our process or our business if that particular element was gone.

307
00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:47.330
John Talburt: How would it impact our processes? How much would it cost to go recover that? And so forth.

308
00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:52.680
John Talburt: But now, the other three things, besides the data, are the people.

309
00:37:54.350 --> 00:38:05.729
John Talburt: The people that are using the data, the people that are manipulating the data, that are transforming the data into some product or service, we can't leave the people out.

310
00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:14.809
John Talburt: Oftentimes, many of the issues that we have are people issues, right? It's the reason we have this course here called Project and Change Management.

311
00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:23.970
John Talburt: Where change managers, about how do you change people's thinking about data and information and how it's being used.

312
00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:32.869
John Talburt: So, in addition to the data, we also need to be very aware of the people that are processing the data, that are using the data, that are managing the data.

313
00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:39.739
John Talburt: Also, what are the processes that are being used to transform the data into some product or service?

314
00:38:39.940 --> 00:38:42.159
John Talburt: We need to understand those processes.

315
00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:47.609
John Talburt: And then finally, we need to understand the technology, what particular software is being used.

316
00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:51.540
John Talburt: Now, this is different than the process, right? We have a process

317
00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:54.060
John Talburt: Of how we want to change the data.

318
00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:56.859
John Talburt: But the tools that we use to change it

319
00:38:57.220 --> 00:39:13.499
John Talburt: can vary, right? So we might be using IBM, we might be using Informatica, SAP, and they're… the way they execute processes are different, and some might be more efficient than others for particular types of processes that we're applying to the data.

320
00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:28.819
John Talburt: So, we always need to keep in mind, in any data scenario, anything related to data, there's obviously the data we need to consider, but we also need to consider the processes, the technology, and the people that are involved.

321
00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:31.930
John Talburt: Those are the four principal components.

322
00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:35.950
John Talburt: Of the context of any, data issue.

323
00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:52.859
John Talburt: And, as we said before, there may be lots of opportunities to make improvements in different ways. So again, this is where the ROI and the other thing, the business cases, come into consideration. Which one should we,

324
00:39:53.260 --> 00:40:00.169
John Talburt: go after. We know that for each opportunity, it has some value, which is the benefits less the cost.

325
00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:08.359
John Talburt: And again, benefits could mean a lot of things, increasing revenue, lowering operational costs, avoiding

326
00:40:08.650 --> 00:40:10.989
John Talburt: Risk of fines and penalties.

327
00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.709
John Talburt: And, cost might be mean…

328
00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:18.809
John Talburt: additional software or additional personnel. So it could be lots of things.

329
00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:23.839
John Talburt: But we try to gather those together and estimate those to weigh the alternatives.

330
00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:29.819
John Talburt: And we often use what's called a cost-benefit matrix.

331
00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:37.760
John Talburt: So, we're going to build a two-dimensional matrix where the vertical, the y-axis, represents the benefit

332
00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:42.080
John Talburt: And the x-axis, or the horizontal axis, represents the cost.

333
00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:47.000
John Talburt: And where we want to place these opportunities are in these quadrants.

334
00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:59.889
John Talburt: So, the upper left quadrant, we place those opportunities that have a high benefit and a low cost. That means that would be our first choice, right? We can get a good benefit, but it doesn't cost us much to get it.

335
00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:02.519
John Talburt: So those would be top-ranked ones.

336
00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:10.430
John Talburt: The second ranked, we have a choice here. We could go down and say, well.

337
00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:20.640
John Talburt: we… we want low cost, but we're not going to get so much benefit. But we always want to try to get the most benefit that we can. So really, our second choice is this quadrant.

338
00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:25.370
John Talburt: This is where we get high benefit, but it costs us more to get that benefit.

339
00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:31.070
John Talburt: But it's important, it's a second choice, because it's a high benefit overall.

340
00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:35.499
John Talburt: Then our third choice would be here. This is where the benefit is lower.

341
00:41:35.660 --> 00:41:41.690
John Talburt: But the cost is low, so this is an easy one in both in terms of cost and benefit.

342
00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:53.840
John Talburt: And that would be our third choice. And then our last choice would be those that the overall benefit is not that great, and it's going to cost us quite a bit to get there, but we will get some positive benefit out of it.

343
00:41:54.130 --> 00:42:00.459
John Talburt: So that would be our first choice. So this gives us a way to map out different opportunities that we have.

344
00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:02.949
John Talburt: To create value with information.

345
00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:08.019
John Talburt: But we want to write up a business case.

346
00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:19.399
John Talburt: And qualitative measures are useful, but it also… but it's really important to try to include some quantitative financial justification. If it's about value.

347
00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:27.699
John Talburt: Then we either need to show that we're gonna make more money out of it, or we're gonna have a lot more benefit for,

348
00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.550
John Talburt: It's say it's medical.

349
00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:33.210
John Talburt: torrible.

350
00:42:33.820 --> 00:42:47.299
John Talburt: That it's gonna… it's gonna be very important in treating some disease or some condition, or if it's a mission incompetence, really gonna help us complete our mission in a much better way than we were doing it before.

351
00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:53.570
John Talburt: So, we need some quantitative, how to express those quantitatively, if we can at all.

352
00:42:54.050 --> 00:42:56.969
John Talburt: And so, then that…

353
00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:05.619
John Talburt: Needs to clearly explain how the benefits of the project is going to exceed the cost that it requires to get that.

354
00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:20.000
John Talburt: That is, the project will create value, so we want to sell that project to man. We want to say, this is an investment you need to make. It's not just a cost that we need to undertake, it's an investment in our organization.

355
00:43:21.150 --> 00:43:23.180
John Talburt: So, here's an example.

356
00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:32.750
John Talburt: qualitative type case would be to say, well, okay, we need to do Project A because it's going to improve

357
00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:40.160
John Talburt: our information product, some X product, that… and that will increase customer satisfaction.

358
00:43:41.230 --> 00:43:47.290
John Talburt: That's very qualitative, because there's no numbers there, there's no percentage, nothing, it just says.

359
00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:53.140
John Talburt: This is a good thing to do. We need to improve that because it's going to make our customers happier.

360
00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:55.230
John Talburt: Yeah, so we should do it.

361
00:43:55.540 --> 00:44:00.399
John Talburt: Mmm, well, okay. So, you're wanting me to spend money for this, okay?

362
00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:05.220
John Talburt: Whereas we look at it more, quantitatively.

363
00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:16.040
John Talburt: Okay, so yeah, this project's gonna improve customer satisfaction, but in a way that we expect that it will increase customer retention by 10% a year.

364
00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:23.949
John Talburt: In other words, we'll lose… we won't lose as many customers each year as we do now. We're going to improve that loss rate.

365
00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:26.390
John Talburt: We're gonna reduce it.

366
00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:28.380
John Talburt: So, by 10%,

367
00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:45.570
John Talburt: And now the cost… and so this is something that accountants in a business are always going to know. They're going to say, well, we know the cost to acquire a new customer. We have to do advertising, we have to do onboarding, we have to do a lot of things to get the customer in to our customer base.

368
00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:49.729
John Talburt: And we estimate that to cost $1,200 a customer.

369
00:44:49.870 --> 00:45:03.670
John Talburt: For a new customer. However, to service and maintain an existing customer, to do their accounts, to do their billing, all that only costs us about $400 a year, okay?

370
00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:09.190
John Talburt: So now, if I have 2,000 customers currently in my business.

371
00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:16.089
John Talburt: then I can make a calculation of how… what would be the impact of having,

372
00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:21.020
John Talburt: Increasing our customer retention by 10% a year.

373
00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:39.420
John Talburt: So for 2,000 customers, I go through the formula. I've got 2,000 customers, I've got 10% retention, I've got the difference between, acquiring them and losing them is $800, right? $1,200 minus the $400.

374
00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:42.520
John Talburt: That's $160,000 a year.

375
00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:46.039
John Talburt: So, I can use that to say.

376
00:45:46.220 --> 00:46:02.650
John Talburt: Okay, if my cost of doing this, project is less than $160,000, then this is a winning formula, right? Because I can say, well, we're going to spend $50,000 to do this, but we're going to get $160,000 back.

377
00:46:05.990 --> 00:46:12.650
John Talburt: So, again, the point of this is it's important, even though we're making estimates, or guesstimates sometimes.

378
00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:26.229
John Talburt: it's important to have the numbers there. And in business, they call this a pro forma, right? In other words, I'm going to start a new business, or I'm proposing a project. I prepare this and say, okay, over the years, over time.

379
00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:34.590
John Talburt: How much am I going to be spending? What do I expect to get back? Show me the money. Show me the money that's going to come back to me by doing this.

380
00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:41.269
John Talburt: So we consider all the factors. In addition to analyzing benefit and cost.

381
00:46:41.450 --> 00:46:44.259
John Talburt: We need to think about how much time that will take.

382
00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:51.240
John Talburt: Will it have a long-term benefit? Is it just something that's gonna be a short-term fix, or a long-term…

383
00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:59.129
John Talburt: What's the risk of failing? Okay, we say we're going to do this project, but could it possibly fail and we not get any return on it?

384
00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:11.800
John Talburt: And do we have the right resources to do it? Are there enough people in the right roles to carry out the project? Do we have the right IT structure, the right data experts, project leaders, so forth?

385
00:47:12.500 --> 00:47:20.300
John Talburt: So, and then also, how visible would the project be? How will people really see the results and understand this?

386
00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:25.619
John Talburt: And that's something we don't do a lot in information quality, sort of blow our own horn.

387
00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:28.899
John Talburt: To say, you know, look what we were able to do for the company.

388
00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:33.370
John Talburt: So that brings us down to,

389
00:47:34.900 --> 00:47:40.429
John Talburt: Some of the characteristics of information, this was going to get us into our next, project.

390
00:47:40.770 --> 00:47:42.420
John Talburt: Our next assignment.

391
00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:50.949
John Talburt: So, the information life cycle. Now, so, information, just like everything, has a life cycle to it.

392
00:47:51.550 --> 00:47:56.140
John Talburt: it starts out somewhere, we acquire the data.

393
00:47:56.280 --> 00:48:02.910
John Talburt: We… we get it, we use it, and at some point, it's not useful to us anymore, okay?

394
00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:06.840
John Talburt: And some data lasts longer than other data.

395
00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:14.110
John Talburt: For example, We think about timeliness of Stock quotes, right?

396
00:48:15.540 --> 00:48:26.480
John Talburt: stock quotes don't last for only a few seconds, right? Because they're changing constantly. So what good does it do for you to give me yesterday's stock quotes? That's not what the stock's worth today, right?

397
00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:29.789
John Talburt: So the life cycle of stock quotes is very, very short.

398
00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:36.050
John Talburt: Other information, like, You know, customer information.

399
00:48:36.220 --> 00:48:41.810
John Talburt: We used to… I used to work at Axiom Corporation. We used to kid each other. We would buy a lot of information.

400
00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:45.979
John Talburt: And we'd say, well, you know, how many times do we need to buy somebody's date of birth?

401
00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:48.160
John Talburt: Well, it doesn't typically change.

402
00:48:48.610 --> 00:49:03.849
John Talburt: So, that has a very long life cycle. As long as that person is potentially a customer or a patient, then their date of birth is a valuable piece of information that we can use to understand their age and other things about their…

403
00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:06.069
John Talburt: About our customer.

404
00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:13.029
John Talburt: So, so life cycle, all data has life cycle, but it varies in length, but it all has…

405
00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:16.430
John Talburt: Certain characteristics or stages that it goes through.

406
00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:24.720
John Talburt: The first part is to plan for the information, before we ever get the information. Is this the information that we even need?

407
00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.139
John Talburt: Why are we getting this information?

408
00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:45.060
John Talburt: And how much is it going to cost us to acquire the information? We think about how, okay, this is information that we want, or data that we want. How do we get it? Is it data that we're going to have to compile ourselves? Are we going to get it from other sources and have to transform it some way?

409
00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:52.900
John Talburt: Are we going to lease it? Are we just going to borrow the data and lease it? A lot of the information we used at Action

410
00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:58.940
John Talburt: was licensed information. Really, we didn't really buy it, we just leased it and used it.

411
00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:04.659
John Talburt: And they kept giving us fresher and fresher information, over time, so it's like a service.

412
00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:13.039
John Talburt: And then, once we get the information, we've got to do something with it. We've got to store it.

413
00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:15.529
John Talburt: They're fuming, and

414
00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:20.699
John Talburt: share it, right? People need… the people that need it need to have access to it.

415
00:50:20.810 --> 00:50:26.570
John Talburt: So we have to figure out how we're going to store it, how we're going to make it available to the people that are going to be using it.

416
00:50:26.870 --> 00:50:32.280
John Talburt: And then we have to maintain it. Generally, stock quotes aside.

417
00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:35.800
John Talburt: If we're talking about, like, customer information.

418
00:50:36.790 --> 00:50:44.849
John Talburt: People move, right? So, if we're going to contact them at their address, whether that's a physical address, or an email address.

419
00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:46.860
John Talburt: Or some social tag.

420
00:50:46.990 --> 00:51:00.750
John Talburt: or some phone number, those tend to change, right? And so to maintain that information, we need to keep up with that and be sure that we have the most current information that we need for whatever product or service that we're building.

421
00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:05.499
John Talburt: So that's important. Then, of course, at some point, we want to use it.

422
00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:12.409
John Talburt: We want to build some information product or service that's going to create that value that we're looking for.

423
00:51:12.790 --> 00:51:24.160
John Talburt: And and then at some point, we're going to dispose. We won't need it anymore, right? We might want to archive it, or we might act… in some cases, we may be required to actually get rid of it, delete it.

424
00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:37.859
John Talburt: certain financial information. The government says you must retain it for 7 years, but at the end of 7 years, you must destroy it. So, all of those are regulatory things we have to be aware of.

425
00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:41.559
John Talburt: And you'll notice that there's 6 things here.

426
00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:48.250
John Talburt: All 6 of those cost. There's cost involved with all of those.

427
00:51:49.150 --> 00:51:51.510
John Talburt: However, only one of them

428
00:51:52.430 --> 00:52:03.220
John Talburt: brings us back any benefit, any value. And that's the apply, right? Only when we get to the point that we're actually using that data for some purpose.

429
00:52:03.250 --> 00:52:12.750
John Talburt: and getting some value out of it, we're getting some return on it. So that's a heavy burden to bear. The apply phase has to carry all other phases.

430
00:52:13.100 --> 00:52:23.220
John Talburt: All other faces cost without giving us any benefit, but the apply is the only place we get benefit. So that's the reason, again, for a business case. We need to be very sure

431
00:52:23.370 --> 00:52:28.280
John Talburt: That if we're going to undertake a project, involving data, that

432
00:52:28.500 --> 00:52:37.209
John Talburt: that the return on investment is going to be positive, right? That we're going to get back more than it costs us to do all these things in the life cycle.

433
00:52:37.580 --> 00:52:42.319
John Talburt: I like this one because I have a bad memory, and I can remember this. It's called POSMAD.

434
00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:43.620
John Talburt: You take the…

435
00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:52.560
John Talburt: first letters of each one of those, plan, obtain, store, maintain, apply, and dispose. POSMAT. And I borrowed that from

436
00:52:53.020 --> 00:53:01.830
John Talburt: Danette McGilray, who's very big in the data quality field, she has a book out called Executing Data Quality Projects.

437
00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:04.610
John Talburt: Very well regarded in the industry.

438
00:53:07.710 --> 00:53:22.000
John Talburt: So again, it's not a linear process. Even though I listed them in order. If we look at it sort of in a two-dimensional way, certainly we start with planning and obtaining it till we get the data. But then, once we get there, we store it and share it.

439
00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:26.419
John Talburt: Then maintaining and applying could be in a cycle, right?

440
00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:31.719
John Talburt: So we need to be constantly maintaining the data, gross data requires some maintenance.

441
00:53:31.820 --> 00:53:34.850
John Talburt: And then we're applying it periodically, right?

442
00:53:35.150 --> 00:53:42.200
John Talburt: We're building every month, or we're standing up a website and people are using it in real time, 24 hours a day.

443
00:53:42.490 --> 00:53:50.560
John Talburt: And applying where they're using that data that we're providing, and we're constantly maintaining. So it's not linear in that sense.

444
00:53:50.770 --> 00:53:54.310
John Talburt: Although, at some point, then, we need to dispose of it, so…

445
00:53:54.580 --> 00:53:57.269
John Talburt: The life cycle's not linear.

446
00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:05.049
John Talburt: the costs, vary, and the application of the data vary, too. And actually, we could be getting

447
00:54:05.300 --> 00:54:15.160
John Talburt: for example, at Action, we were getting data all the time. We planned for data, but then once we got a license, they would give us a feed every… typically every month, sometimes every week.

448
00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:24.300
John Talburt: That would have to be stored, we'd have to maintain it, apply it constantly. So, this is a very cyclical, and not just a linear process.

449
00:54:25.990 --> 00:54:33.040
John Talburt: And so again, I won't go into a lot of detail on all these, because we're close.

450
00:54:33.170 --> 00:54:40.169
John Talburt: But, again, it's very important to prepare for getting data.

451
00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:49.889
John Talburt: For example, again, using the Axiom example, a lot of the data that we would get, which was licensed data or, lease data.

452
00:54:50.320 --> 00:55:00.960
John Talburt: had restrictions on it. We could only use it for certain things, so it didn't do us any good to pay $20,000 to get a data feed to create some kind of a index

453
00:55:01.170 --> 00:55:12.500
John Talburt: that wasn't allowed for… in the data. The data could, for example, only be used for marketing. It couldn't be used, to build some kind of reference data that we would use over and over.

454
00:55:12.710 --> 00:55:18.489
John Talburt: So, you have to be aware of what any restrictions that might be on the data in your planning.

455
00:55:18.620 --> 00:55:23.120
John Talburt: And, you know, what's the condition? We would always ask vendors

456
00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:29.710
John Talburt: to give us samples for certain zip codes. We were mostly using demographic data, name and address at that time.

457
00:55:29.930 --> 00:55:35.489
John Talburt: And so we would say, okay, yeah, but okay, we're interested in buying this or leasing this data.

458
00:55:35.890 --> 00:55:39.170
John Talburt: But we need a sample to see what's the quality of it.

459
00:55:39.280 --> 00:55:46.190
John Talburt: So we had a list of certain zip codes, and we would say, okay, here's a list of zip… give us all the data you have for these zip codes.

460
00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:51.139
John Talburt: And that would allow us to look and see, you know, what was the quantity compared to other vendors?

461
00:55:51.260 --> 00:55:59.889
John Talburt: We could look at the data, see what the quality is, and compare vendors, and many vendors in the space selling this, or leasing this data.

462
00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:05.569
John Talburt: So that, that gave us a way to plan and prepare and make a decision. And then,

463
00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:16.800
John Talburt: We also get us ready, you know, to be prepared to receive it, so that if we had to design certain applications or processes to use it.

464
00:56:17.430 --> 00:56:22.540
John Talburt: And again, as I mentioned, what are the limitations or restrictions on the use of it?

465
00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:25.580
John Talburt: And then you actually obtain it.

466
00:56:26.200 --> 00:56:36.130
John Talburt: And you're either going to create, or compiling is actually generating the data yourself, so that was another thing we often did. Even though we leased a lot of data.

467
00:56:36.710 --> 00:56:42.999
John Talburt: We would compile some of our own data, and this was back in the day when people published telephone directories.

468
00:56:43.350 --> 00:56:49.649
John Talburt: So we would take and buy… we bought about 2,000 telephone directories from all over the United States.

469
00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:56.769
John Talburt: Took them to a book bindery, and they cut off the binding to make it all loose-leaf.

470
00:56:57.020 --> 00:57:03.430
John Talburt: And we bundled all the loose-leaf pages of the telephone directory and shipped it to China.

471
00:57:03.820 --> 00:57:09.720
John Talburt: And in China, there would be these young women from the countryside that would come into town.

472
00:57:10.100 --> 00:57:17.480
John Talburt: And there would be these, companies that were set up with laptop computers, back in the old 286 days.

473
00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:23.350
John Talburt: And they would give one operator a page of the directory.

474
00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:31.489
John Talburt: And she would go in, and she didn't have to understand English, just that here's an A on the page, and here's an A on the keyboard.

475
00:57:31.640 --> 00:57:34.390
John Talburt: Of typing in all of that information.

476
00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:41.649
John Talburt: Then they would take the same page, go over to a second operator, let the second operator key in that page.

477
00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:55.149
John Talburt: And then they would take the two keying operations and compare them, and that way they could… we could get almost 100% accuracy in the keying, because any discrepancy would go to a third person who would reconcile and get the correct values.

478
00:57:55.670 --> 00:58:02.930
John Talburt: So, that's called compiling the… you're generating the information yourself.

479
00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:08.500
John Talburt: We did that some, but we also bought or, leased or rented a lot of data.

480
00:58:09.460 --> 00:58:15.810
John Talburt: and you have to load those files. This was another big issue that was very cost-intensive. When I left.

481
00:58:16.230 --> 00:58:24.359
John Talburt: the company, there were 825 employees That their only job

482
00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:29.469
John Talburt: was to take, in that case, it's mostly a tape reel, a reel of tape, or

483
00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:32.889
John Talburt: In some cases, it's a digital file of data.

484
00:58:33.470 --> 00:58:37.310
John Talburt: And inspect it, and to look and see

485
00:58:37.910 --> 00:58:43.609
John Talburt: How… what were the fields in the data, and were they aligned properly?

486
00:58:43.980 --> 00:58:47.470
John Talburt: Because in our operation.

487
00:58:47.630 --> 00:58:56.089
John Talburt: however the data came in, it had to be mapped to a standard format, and so none of the data we were getting was already in that format.

488
00:58:56.200 --> 00:59:10.150
John Talburt: So we… so somebody had to look at it, inspect it, make a decision of how they were going to transform the data from the way it was received into the standard format that we wanted to use. And we were getting much… lots and lots of data

489
00:59:10.150 --> 00:59:20.850
John Talburt: So it took more than 800 people to do that job every day of getting those files, looking at them, how is it aligned, and how are they going to be transformed into the standard format.

490
00:59:24.880 --> 00:59:35.569
John Talburt: And, and so basically, a lot of our compiling was, well, semi-structured, so telephone directories are semi-structured, they're not fully structured.

491
00:59:35.910 --> 00:59:44.749
John Talburt: But then you have a lot of unstructured data documents, for example, one of the things we were interested in

492
00:59:45.680 --> 00:59:53.020
John Talburt: were, corporate actions, and so the federal government requires corporations to file,

493
00:59:53.210 --> 00:59:57.290
John Talburt: Documentation when there's some major change in their business.

494
00:59:57.730 --> 01:00:00.190
John Talburt: Well, for example, change of ownership, or…

495
01:00:00.380 --> 01:00:08.820
John Talburt: Things like that. Then they filed a certain form, but that form was a narrative form. It wasn't a fielded form.

496
01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:18.709
John Talburt: you just wrote in what had happened, and you could describe it. So, getting the information of who bought whom, which company bought which other company, and when.

497
01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:24.650
John Talburt: Out of that required reading that document somehow, and structuring that information.

498
01:00:24.770 --> 01:00:36.679
John Talburt: So that is a big issue that we're having now, and that's one of the major applications we're using for AI now, because now large language models can read and understand

499
01:00:36.890 --> 01:00:47.210
John Talburt: So rather than having a person try to read a document and pull out information, we can now use large language models to read that data and identify information and pull it out.

500
01:00:47.770 --> 01:00:52.670
John Talburt: So that's one of the major applications that we have right now of large language models.

501
01:00:53.750 --> 01:00:58.670
John Talburt: And it can be applied also to images and to audio as well.

502
01:00:59.150 --> 01:01:03.200
John Talburt: Even right now, I don't know, in this,

503
01:01:03.820 --> 01:01:09.440
John Talburt: recording, I don't know, when you read the recording, you can see a subtitle, right? It's changing…

504
01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:14.099
John Talburt: My voice and my… what… my narrative of the,

505
01:01:14.420 --> 01:01:18.400
John Talburt: that I'm talking… whatever I'm talking about, into,

506
01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:21.550
John Talburt: Text, right? So, audio to text.

507
01:01:22.200 --> 01:01:27.029
John Talburt: And you go, same thing, image detect, with OCR.

508
01:01:29.460 --> 01:01:31.350
John Talburt: Okay, well,

509
01:01:33.270 --> 01:01:43.639
John Talburt: Yeah, we're still good on that. So, then again, you know, you have to store it and share it, and that's an operation all of itself, of taking and designing

510
01:01:44.070 --> 01:01:56.310
John Talburt: If you're using a relational database, now we don't use that as much as we did. We don't worry as much about normalizing the data for big data operations. But still, we have to store it in some way.

511
01:01:56.800 --> 01:01:59.059
John Talburt: And make a way to share it.

512
01:01:59.460 --> 01:02:09.189
John Talburt: And that's a double-edged sword, right? We have to make it available. If people are not using it, we're not getting any value. But we have to be careful with whom we share it, right? Because

513
01:02:09.400 --> 01:02:13.650
John Talburt: We don't want to make it so available that anybody can come in and pick up our data.

514
01:02:14.210 --> 01:02:16.280
John Talburt: And have data leakage.

515
01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:28.380
John Talburt: And then we've already talked about maintaining it, so that… ensure that it stays up to date and everything continues to work properly. Many operations that we often do.

516
01:02:28.590 --> 01:02:33.489
John Talburt: To change… parsing is a big thing, where we have fields that are

517
01:02:33.790 --> 01:02:42.940
John Talburt: Like, a person's name, and we want to parse it out so that we separate out the first name, the middle name, last name, generational suffix, things like that.

518
01:02:43.350 --> 01:02:50.979
John Talburt: Standardize it so that Street always conforms to a certain abbreviation, ST, that the Postal Service likes.

519
01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:57.040
John Talburt: when there's a lot of people who put STR or spell out street,

520
01:02:57.160 --> 01:03:03.669
John Talburt: So there's a lot of things that need to be done to maintain it, to put it in, and then to keep it up to date.

521
01:03:04.300 --> 01:03:17.520
John Talburt: And then we may want to enhance it. Oftentimes, we would take data, and we wanted to know the gender, right? So, there's lots of tools that will take and look at names and try to decide what the gender

522
01:03:17.690 --> 01:03:20.390
John Talburt: Associated with that name.

523
01:03:20.870 --> 01:03:23.990
John Talburt: And then a big one for us is deduplication.

524
01:03:24.120 --> 01:03:33.020
John Talburt: That's my main research area, is how do I… when I'm getting duplicates of the same information, it may not be an exact duplicate.

525
01:03:33.320 --> 01:03:40.000
John Talburt: It may be John, I mean, it may be Bill Smith, and another one may be William Smith, spelled differently, a nickname.

526
01:03:40.180 --> 01:03:45.160
John Talburt: But can I identify that this is really the same Bill or William Smith?

527
01:03:45.300 --> 01:03:51.940
John Talburt: The same customer or the same patient. And deduplicate and gather those together that, match.

528
01:03:53.040 --> 01:04:01.759
John Talburt: And then merge together. And so, again, looking at the hospital situation, I have all of these patient encounters.

529
01:04:01.910 --> 01:04:08.450
John Talburt: I need to identify which of those encounters are for the same patient, merge those together to build a medical chart.

530
01:04:09.060 --> 01:04:20.569
John Talburt: So that I know from all the different visits that the patient has made, what have been all the diagnostic tests, what have been all the recommendations, the prescriptions, and so forth that were made.

531
01:04:20.700 --> 01:04:22.710
John Talburt: Into one, bring it all together.

532
01:04:23.390 --> 01:04:33.710
John Talburt: And then apply, of course, is the main reason we're doing all of it. We're trying to build something, some product or service, that's going to create value, either internally or externally.

533
01:04:34.440 --> 01:04:43.010
John Talburt: The main product that we were building at Axiom… well, there's two main products, but one of the main products is that we were building an enhancement database.

534
01:04:43.170 --> 01:04:46.800
John Talburt: By putting together all the information about consumers.

535
01:04:47.280 --> 01:04:56.579
John Talburt: Things like, you know, were they married? Did they own a house, or were they renting? What kind of car do they own? All these many things. What were their hobbies? What were their interests?

536
01:04:56.900 --> 01:05:01.960
John Talburt: And not… all of it didn't come from one place, so we got it from…

537
01:05:02.130 --> 01:05:04.530
John Talburt: Probably about 80 different sources.

538
01:05:04.780 --> 01:05:15.289
John Talburt: And then when it would come in, then the first thing we'd have to do is decide, are we talking about the same person? And if we are, then how do we merge together? Because sometimes there'd be disagreement.

539
01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:21.140
John Talburt: Some sources would say, yes, this person owns a house. Another source would say, no, they're renting a house.

540
01:05:21.250 --> 01:05:30.330
John Talburt: And so we'd have to make decisions about which we felt was the most correct. Was it true they were just at different times, or was one incorrect?

541
01:05:30.810 --> 01:05:43.159
John Talburt: So, making all those decisions and merging those together to create, and so then what we had is for any particular customer, we could tell you about a thousand different things about that customer.

542
01:05:43.450 --> 01:05:53.179
John Talburt: And so people would come in and buy that, because banks especially would want to know, okay, we have this customer in our bank, but all we know is their name and their address.

543
01:05:53.850 --> 01:06:04.869
John Talburt: You tell us whether they own the house, or what kind of car, so we'll know how to build a model whether to offer them this kind of credit card, or this kind of credit card, or not to offer them a credit card.

544
01:06:05.280 --> 01:06:06.889
John Talburt: So,

545
01:06:07.100 --> 01:06:14.340
John Talburt: That was the… one of the large products, information products that we built was this enhancement database.

546
01:06:14.660 --> 01:06:16.850
John Talburt: But there were other things we did as well.

547
01:06:17.520 --> 01:06:21.700
John Talburt: Other types of products and services, so…

548
01:06:23.120 --> 01:06:25.759
John Talburt: Lots of things. And then, of course, dispose.

549
01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:37.269
John Talburt: Typically, as I say, we often just archive it, we put it aside, because we may need it later, but in some cases, regulations require us for us to destroy the information.

550
01:06:39.790 --> 01:06:46.360
John Talburt: So, life cycle value, cost, and quality, as I mentioned earlier.

551
01:06:46.480 --> 01:06:51.810
John Talburt: All of those phases require some cost to do, but only the apply phase

552
01:06:52.420 --> 01:06:58.009
John Talburt: Creates value, or creates benefits, and so potentially creates value for us.

553
01:06:59.070 --> 01:07:06.480
John Talburt: So, and the information quality can be affected by activities in all of these phases.

554
01:07:06.810 --> 01:07:15.700
John Talburt: So, information's a unique resource. As a raw material, it's not consumed. So, remember we said that a lot of what we have in information quality

555
01:07:15.930 --> 01:07:17.769
John Talburt: Comes from manufacturing.

556
01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:28.250
John Talburt: And so that's a good analogy. It's like, you know, we've got an information factory, we get raw products, or raw materials, which are the raw data.

557
01:07:28.360 --> 01:07:39.880
John Talburt: We put it through machinery, which is the programs and systems that we run, and then our end goal is to create some information, product, or service that we can sell or create value out of.

558
01:07:41.220 --> 01:07:48.619
John Talburt: And so, it's like a manufacturing operation when we do that. However, the analogy kind of falls down on two places.

559
01:07:49.030 --> 01:07:53.759
John Talburt: that we're going to talk quite a bit about. One important place where it falls down is

560
01:07:54.150 --> 01:07:58.820
John Talburt: That, in a real manufacturing process,

561
01:07:59.030 --> 01:08:04.890
John Talburt: When we… when we start to build something, we have tolerances

562
01:08:05.270 --> 01:08:17.279
John Talburt: For the parts, right? We're gonna build a car, we have certain diameter requirements for the axle, certain tensile strength, certain… in other words, and those understanding of those tolerances

563
01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:27.120
John Talburt: is pretty intuitive. We understand the weight of something, we understand its tensile strength, we understand how big it is, how much it weighs.

564
01:08:27.370 --> 01:08:40.079
John Talburt: You know, how long it is, what its diameter, all of those are physical things that we can understand. But when it comes to data, the characteristics of data, those dimensions of data, data quality.

565
01:08:40.189 --> 01:08:42.320
John Talburt: Are not as obvious, right?

566
01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:45.509
John Talburt: Timeliness is not as obvious.

567
01:08:45.680 --> 01:08:50.770
John Talburt: As the diameter of some, a piece of equipment.

568
01:08:51.090 --> 01:08:54.819
John Talburt: The other place, and this is what made me think of it, this slide.

569
01:08:55.420 --> 01:09:02.000
John Talburt: Is that when we're building something, like a car, or a toaster, or whatever we're building, a manufactured product.

570
01:09:02.670 --> 01:09:11.639
John Talburt: the raw materials that we bring in are consumed, right? We build cars, we're using up aluminum, we're using up steel, we build more cars, we have to buy more.

571
01:09:12.720 --> 01:09:15.529
John Talburt: That's the good and the bad thing about data.

572
01:09:15.760 --> 01:09:30.989
John Talburt: is that once we get data, we can use it over and over. We can copy, we can use as many copies as we want, endlessly, right? An infinite amount. We're not using up the data when we process it in a program, it doesn't get used up.

573
01:09:31.740 --> 01:09:47.819
John Talburt: That's a good thing in that it increases our marginal cost, right? If we can get data one time and sell copies of it a thousand times, then that's a great thing. We can get huge marginal increases on the… and the more we sell, the more we get.

574
01:09:47.930 --> 01:09:51.250
John Talburt: However, the downside of that is if it's bad.

575
01:09:51.810 --> 01:09:55.630
John Talburt: And we replicate bad data and get it out there, then it's…

576
01:09:55.780 --> 01:10:07.320
John Talburt: You know, some people say that, like, data is the new oil. And one argument is that, no, it's not like oil, because when you spill oil, you can clean it up.

577
01:10:07.840 --> 01:10:15.970
John Talburt: But when you spill data, and your data gets out there, you can never get it back, right? So if it's bad data, or if it's sensitive data, and it goes out.

578
01:10:16.110 --> 01:10:17.530
John Talburt: It's gone.

579
01:10:17.900 --> 01:10:19.739
John Talburt: And you can't get it back.

580
01:10:22.110 --> 01:10:24.640
John Talburt: So, raw material's not consumed.

581
01:10:25.650 --> 01:10:32.200
John Talburt: and easily copy it and use it over and over again. So quality's critical. Copies of bad data are bad data.

582
01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:37.709
John Talburt: However, the advantage is the marginal value increases the more we use it.

583
01:10:38.850 --> 01:10:44.550
John Talburt: And it's not a fungible asset. I don't know if you're familiar with the idea of fungibility in finance.

584
01:10:44.840 --> 01:10:49.740
John Talburt: But fungible assets are those that are interchangeable.

585
01:10:50.310 --> 01:10:56.290
John Talburt: So, if I have certain… If I have barrels of oil, right, a certain type of crude oil.

586
01:10:56.560 --> 01:11:04.040
John Talburt: There are different types of crude oil, but if they're the same type of crude oil, one barrel of oil is just like another barrel of oil.

587
01:11:04.680 --> 01:11:07.230
John Talburt: The barrels don't have identity to them.

588
01:11:07.400 --> 01:11:10.149
John Talburt: But that's generally not true with data.

589
01:11:10.270 --> 01:11:20.730
John Talburt: Data tends to be non-fungible, in the sense that we're describing entities like customers, like patients, like products.

590
01:11:20.980 --> 01:11:25.319
John Talburt: And those are all unique and separately identifiable.

591
01:11:28.630 --> 01:11:30.450
John Talburt: So they're not interchangeable.

592
01:11:31.560 --> 01:11:37.620
John Talburt: So, I think it's probably a good place to end today's lecture. So, I'll be giving you

593
01:11:38.020 --> 01:11:45.350
John Talburt: That data, we'll have an assignment coming up where we're going to start working with some data and looking at the quality of it.

594
01:11:45.870 --> 01:11:49.919
John Talburt: Any quick questions or comments before we leave today?

595
01:11:53.690 --> 01:11:54.540
John Talburt: Okay.

596
01:11:55.440 --> 01:11:59.300
John Talburt: If not, I'm going to… Stop. I do.

